WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:20.979 --> 00:00:22.270
JELENA PJESIVAC-GRBOVIC: Hello.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.420
Hello, everyone, and welcome.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.710
Hadoop and its ecosystem has
become increasingly important

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.470
to businesses of every type.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.700
Hadoop holds the
promise that it will

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.980
unlock the value of
your data and give you

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.030
the insights that will help
you flourish your business.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:43.240
Now, you're here, so
maybe you're aware of,

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:47.020
or you personally experienced,
that the overheads of managing

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:53.760
Hadoop infrastructures
are not so small.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.120
As the number and
sophistication of the workloads

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.690
we run increases, so does
the operational overhead

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.990
of our clusters too.

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:07.410
This increase in the overhead
undermines the core value

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.110
proposition that Hadoop
brings to the table.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.570
Essentially, it takes
away focus from the data

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.220
onto how to run the cluster.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.850
This is where Google
Cloud Platform and Cloud

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.830
Dataproc come in.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.990
My name is Jelena
Pjesivac-Grbovic.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.190
I'm tech leader
manager at Google.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.140
And today, we will see how
your migration to cloud

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.120
and the incremental
changes to the way

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.140
you run your Hadoop
and Spark workloads

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.030
can let you preserve the power
of the open source system

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:40.620
that you've built and reduce
the operational complexity,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.500
allowing you to
refocus on the data.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.610
This is a journey.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:48.640
Migration to the cloud
doesn't have a one way

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.420
that works for everyone.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.020
There are many
valid paths to take.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.500
We will highlight some
overarching guidelines

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.520
and the principles, and identify
useful building blocks that

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.240
will make the migration to
cloud easier and smoother

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.530
and allow you to benefit
earlier from the new stack.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.880
So let's begin with what
is Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.170
Google Cloud Dataproc is managed
Hadoop and Spark service.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.690
Its goal is to help you
refocus your efforts

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.080
to your data instead of
managing the cluster.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.820
Dataproc achieves this by being
fast, easy, and cost effective.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:32.910
What do I mean by that?

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.320
Imagine a new version
of Hadoop is coming out.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.470
It promises to make
your critical workload

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.270
10 times faster.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.950
Sounds great, right?

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.910
What would it take for you
to deploy this new version

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.700
of Hadoop in your data center?

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:53.270
Think of the testing,
validation, qualification,

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.830
and everything else.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.630
But Dataproc-- this would
be a one line command,

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.910
or alternatively a few clicks.

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.380
About 90 seconds after
that command line,

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.470
you would have a brand new
data or cluster with a version

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:15.910
that you want, and you would be
ready to canary your workloads

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.310
even at the production scale.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.536
The speed and simplicity
at which you can run--

00:03:21.536 --> 00:03:22.890
I don't what's this--

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.140
at which you can run
and start the clusters

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:28.030
lends itself to
cost effectiveness,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.820
because you can run the
cluster and the resources

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:32.820
only when you need them.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.670
There is a whole
spectrum of approaches

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.980
on running Hadoop on GCP.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.820
Starting with an
on-premise, right, we

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.570
can have any of
these options there.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.570
And where you're on
the spectrum depends on

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:54.440
whether you treat GCP as
infrastructure as a service

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.650
or as a platform as a service.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:03.260
Along the spectrum, GCP
works with many partners that

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.870
will help your
migration, and you don't

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:07.220
have to be alone on this path.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:14.320
So lets look at how the Dataproc
works and how's it is built.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.579
Dataproc layers the features
on top of the standard Hadoop

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:18.670
stack.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:23.470
Between the Hadoop layers,
we insert the monitoring,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.070
statuses, configuration
options, things like that,

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:32.270
that augment the Hadoop stack
and make it more manageable.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:37.180
We built our functionality using
native, open source APIs, which

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.650
means that whatever you did
for your own customization

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:46.870
in your own cluster
will work in Dataproc.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.370
You will have a full power--

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.620
how you access these clusters.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.750
You can use your
standard APIs that you're

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.740
used to, like Hadoop
command line-- you know,

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.550
ssh to the master.

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:04.300
Or you can use our Rust APIs
and our UI third party tools

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.800
that we are integrated with.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.200
It's your choice.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.230
Our distro is based
on the Bigtop,

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.750
and it comes with Hadoop,
Spark, Hive, and Pig by default.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.520
They are enabled immediately.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.322
We provide you with ability to
enable Tez, Zeppelin, and Kafka

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:24.280
via something we called
initialization actions.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.590
So imagine you are
in a place where

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.620
you are enticed with a cloud,
and you want to try it out.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.720
So what should be
your next step?

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:39.070
You should be planning
your Proof of Concept, POC.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.890
During the POC, you
will have to think about

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.460
what will it take to move
your on premise solution

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.350
to the cloud.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:49.160
You will think about mechanics.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.040
You know, how can I do this?

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.800
But we encourage
you to think bigger.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.690
You should think about
the opportunities

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.250
to simplify your stack, as well.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.200
And as you proceed
with POC, this design

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.700
is going to be really important.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.120
Concretely, on
thinking the bigger--

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:09.640
in your on-premise
setup, Hadoop clusters

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.180
are likely the core
of your data platform.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.710
In Cloud, they're just one
of the building blocks,

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.570
and you can have
many building blocks

00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:22.090
and many different
building blocks.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:24.350
Example of the building
blocks we'll mention today--

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.170
and you probably
heard today, also,

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:30.820
things like GCS, BigQuery,
Bigtable, Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.400
Stackdriver, machine
learning, right?

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:37.610
All of these can eventually
become parts of your platform

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:41.900
that's maybe just starting with
Dataproc and Hadoop, right?

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.360
So when you think of migration,
you should lift your platform,

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.340
and you should leverage
these things, as well.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:57.210
When starting the POC, you need
to identify your high level

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:59.340
goals for the
migration, and you want

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.170
them to be clear
and well understood.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.170
Some of the goals are on
here, like cost savings.

00:07:06.170 --> 00:07:07.820
What does the cost
saving mean to you?

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:10.220
Is it the total
cost of ownership,

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:13.520
or it is the cost
performance balance?

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.460
Like you want more performance
for the same amount of money.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.220
What you do, depending
on which of these two

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.820
goals you care about more--

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:26.870
it will depend on which of these
two goals you actually have.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:31.040
You have concerns about
the scaling elasticity.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.690
Do you want to
solve that problem

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.390
by having multiple clusters?

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.505
Or you'll go a step
further, and you

00:07:37.505 --> 00:07:40.790
will disaggregate your
storage and compute.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.250
And then you can have as
many clusters as you want,

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:48.530
and you can even throw
different tools on your data.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:53.140
Development agility, like,
how do you handle versioning?

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:56.840
Do you care about heterogeneous
dependencies and et cetera?

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.100
You don't have to--

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.860
when you think of
these goals, you

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.220
don't have to make significant
changes to your stack.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:08.180
But you want to consciously
think of the goals you have

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.260
and do the analysis of--

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.290
concretely do the analysis
of the cost and benefits

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.057
of keeping something
the same or trying

00:08:17.057 --> 00:08:18.140
to change it in a process.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.210
So we now have goals,
which is great,

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.850
but we have to understand
our requirements,

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:32.470
the characteristics of
your current solution,

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.120
existing system, and also
the timeline that you have

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.789
in mind for this migration.

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:40.400
To get to the requirements,
you should look at the tools

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.049
that you're currently using.

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:43.760
What are the components?

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.890
What are the components
you actually want to keep?

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:48.050
Because some of the
components are really

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:50.390
the requirement, and
some of the components

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:52.340
you added because you had to.

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:55.650
It was a hack around the
problem you had, right?

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.290
So perhaps that
hack doesn't have

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:58.910
to go with you to the cloud.

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:02.360
Maybe there is a better
solution there, right?

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.136
Think about capacity planning.

00:09:04.136 --> 00:09:05.510
You're going to
use the Dataproc,

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:09.170
so you're still responsible
for sizing your clusters.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:12.170
So think about how
much compute you need.

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:13.670
What are the average peak?

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:17.730
And what is the peak, and
what's average data throughput

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.130
that you want to support?

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.190
Think of the security models.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.190
And finally, thinking
of the timeline

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:29.030
is important, because depending
the duration of migration

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.950
that you have in
mind, you may have

00:09:30.950 --> 00:09:33.530
to build a hybrid
infrastructure that

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:37.705
will host both your current
on-prem thing and the cloud

00:09:37.705 --> 00:09:38.205
cluster.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.877
OK, so we talked
about the planning.

00:09:45.877 --> 00:09:46.960
You've done your planning.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:47.910
You have your goals.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.160
What is the first step?

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:52.040
The first step will
be the data migration.

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:59.270
So usually when you're
thinking of data migration,

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.260
you look at your storage options
and the types of data you have.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:07.760
At the cloud-- at GCP, we have
quite a few storage solutions.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:09.920
The standard one is
Google Cloud Storage,

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.220
which is our large
scale object store that

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:16.670
is accessible through
File-Based API.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.546
We also gave you two
flavors of the HDFS--

00:10:20.546 --> 00:10:23.450
one based on the
persistent disks and one

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:26.170
on the local SSDs.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:30.380
Then, if you use Hive,
we have a cloud Bigtable,

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:31.640
and we have BigQuery.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:33.710
We also have Spanner
and many other products

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:36.860
that could meet your needs.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.680
So you should evaluate
them, understand

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.410
whatever benefits and
drawbacks are, and then you

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.400
should look at your data.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.430
And usually, one
of the first data

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:50.560
sets that moves to the cloud
is archival and call data--

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:54.650
the stuff you have to take care
of, but you don't really use.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.620
So that's usually the first
thing that moves to the cloud.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:01.100
And then you start moving
parts of the live, hot data,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:05.480
and you start computing with it.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:06.920
You do want to--

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.560
one thing that's important
is how you access the data.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:12.500
So to choose which
storage option you want,

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:16.130
you really want to think
whether the data set requires

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:17.340
random access or not.

00:11:20.288 --> 00:11:22.650
So again, the first movers--

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:23.570
yeah.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:25.430
And one thing to
think about is, like,

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:28.880
if you have to have a
hybrid architecture,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:30.680
you need to figure
out the solution that

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.580
will allow you to keep the
same data set in two places.

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:36.710
And the partners are
really helpful with that.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:50.370
OK, so GCS is usually the first
GCP solution you will use.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:53.760
So I would like to call out
some differences between GCP

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:54.630
and HDFS.

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.670
I'm assuming you're
familiar with the HDFS.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.140
So GCP is our object store.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.930
And for the most
part, you can use it

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.720
as a drop-in
replacement for HDFS.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.330
It has a Hadoop file
system interface.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:14.310
All your I/O connectors based
file, input/output formats,

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:18.701
contexts of text file,
et cetera, just work.

00:12:18.701 --> 00:12:22.320
A nice thing with
GCS connector is also

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:25.000
that you can install it on
your own on-premise cluster.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:32.890
But GCS, you will get the
cleaner architecture, why?

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:35.590
Because the data will live
outside of your cluster.

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:39.100
It will live in our
distributed storage that is

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.380
replicated across the zones--

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:44.440
perhaps multi-regionally
if that's how you want it.

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:47.170
And you can throw
any compute on it.

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:52.000
You you're not limited
to just the compute

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:55.450
that you have in your cluster.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.490
Another nice thing of
the GCS is that there

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:59.860
is no concept of a NameNode.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:03.100
So if you ever experienced
killability issues

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:06.180
in your clusters due to
NameNode performance,

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.050
this does not exist.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.120
There's no such thing here.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:14.540
And you also no longer have
a single point of failure.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.870
To optimize GCS performance,
conveniently, you

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:20.360
use the same approaches
like you optimize HDFS.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:25.690
So you want to optimize for
bulk and parallel operations.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:29.560
So the pros of GCS
are high throughput,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:33.250
no scaling bottlenecks, no
single point of failure,

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:37.590
and the data can be durable
across the zones and regions.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.790
There are some drawbacks.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:41.530
With the fact that
there is no NameNode

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:43.450
and that you're across
the zones and regions,

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:46.280
your latency can go up.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.980
Directories in
GCS are simulated.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.070
So if your application
relies on checking

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:56.240
a timestamp of a directory,
there is no such thing.

00:13:56.240 --> 00:13:58.580
So you either have to
modify the application,

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:02.220
or you have to use HDFS, right?

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:08.540
And objects in GCS cannot be
appended once they're closed.

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:13.760
So these are sort of things
to think about when you want

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:15.140
to move the data sets to GCS.

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:23.140
So, OK, we want to
move the data sets.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:24.410
What approaches can we take?

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:27.380
Well, we can push it,
or we can pull it.

00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:30.070
Those are two, generally,
approaches you can do.

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:31.660
Push approach is--

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.520
I'm in my on-prem cluster.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:36.970
I connect to the
GCS via GCS APIs,

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:40.990
and I copy the data
from my cluster to GCS.

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:41.780
It's simple.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.080
It has a straightforward
security model.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.390
Your data is encrypted
with SSL, and it

00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:49.190
goes through internet
gateways like this.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:51.430
So you are in your
on-prem cluster,

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.590
and you send your data to GCS.

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.500
OK, so that's the
simplest model.

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:58.930
The downside of this
model is that you're

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:02.690
using your compute and on-prem
cluster to move the data.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:06.560
So if you were resource
constraint on your cluster,

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:08.890
this is sort of--

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:12.640
you're using more resources.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:15.080
Alternative model
is a pull model.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:17.920
So in a pull model,
I set up the VPN

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:21.940
such that my cloud cluster
can access the data on my

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:23.740
on premise cluster.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:26.500
You really want to
do this carefully.

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:29.200
And from there you
use the compute

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:34.420
in your cloud to just pull the
data from your on-prem cluster.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.640
From there, the
data can go either

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:42.760
on your HDFS in the cloud, or
to storage, or to BigQuery.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:44.320
It doesn't matter.

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:46.780
All of these are options.

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:50.560
The main difference
in a security model

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:53.200
in the pull approach,
your security

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:57.340
relies on you setting up the
VPN properly and ensuring

00:15:57.340 --> 00:15:59.230
that you can set up
the permissions such

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:01.090
that only you can
access the data.

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:11.900
So let's take a look at
some of the cloud native

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.780
architectural concepts.

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:21.100
On your on-prem cluster, the
data locality was premium.

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:23.560
And essentially, you were
computing over the data

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:25.760
that existed in your cluster.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.330
That was done for performance
reasons and convenience

00:16:28.330 --> 00:16:30.850
reasons, because
usually when you decided

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:32.440
you needed a
petabyte of storage,

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:35.036
you bought a cluster that
had a petabyte of storage,

00:16:35.036 --> 00:16:36.160
and it came with some CPUs.

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:41.500
Your computations come
and go in a cluster,

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:43.760
but your data sits there.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:49.180
So the computation and the data
have very different lifespans

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:50.560
for the most part.

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:52.570
So why not just separate them?

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:54.940
Why do we have to
tie the computation

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.390
to the lifespan of the data?

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:01.540
And the process
of disaggregation

00:17:01.540 --> 00:17:06.490
of storage and compute
is essentially separating

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:09.730
data plane from the computing.

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:12.800
We essentially pull out
the data from the machines.

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:15.609
So if this is our
cluster, we no longer

00:17:15.609 --> 00:17:20.619
keep data inside a cluster, but
we use GCS, BigQuery, Bigtable,

00:17:20.619 --> 00:17:24.220
Spanner, whichever
data storage you chose.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:27.579
And our clusters are
there for computing.

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.780
And when we're done computing,
maybe we can get rid of them.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:35.750
At the same time, we also--

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:39.770
our clusters will produce some
data, such as monitoring data,

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:41.550
and log in data,
and stuff like that.

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:43.820
And then it will also live
outside of the cluster.

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:45.500
It will come out of it.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.690
Because we don't want to keep--

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:51.380
this information
is useful even when

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:55.093
the computation is done so we
separated from the computation.

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:03.410
So this gives you quite
a bit of flexibility

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:05.870
on how you manage
cluster lifespan.

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:07.820
But another thing
that it gives you also

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:10.370
is ability to burst in Cloud.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:11.400
What do I mean by that?

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:17.600
In Cloud, a job, a task, that
will take 1,000 CPU hours

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:23.090
will cost the same if it runs
on 1,000 machines for one hour

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:26.480
or on 10 machines for 100 hours.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:27.770
The math adds up.

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:32.300
The CPU usage, disk usage,
RAM usage adds up, right?

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:34.820
But it's very likely
that that value

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:37.820
is way more valuable to you
if you waited one hour for it

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:40.460
as opposed to 100 hours.

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:43.190
Now, if your data was
confined on the machines that

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:45.170
are computing,
the chances of you

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.500
being able to throw
1,000 machines on it

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:50.307
trivially is low.

00:18:50.307 --> 00:18:52.640
But if you store it on something
that's highly scalable,

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:56.510
like GCS, you can actually
throw this many resources on it

00:18:56.510 --> 00:18:58.910
and compute is much
faster for the same price.

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:09.780
Another feature of
Dataproc and is used in GCP

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:12.620
are preemptible VMs.

00:19:12.620 --> 00:19:17.670
Preemptible VMs are regular VMs,
but Google reclaims the right--

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:20.670
Google keeps the rights
to reclaim them as needed

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:22.014
so they can come and go.

00:19:22.014 --> 00:19:22.930
Usually they're there.

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:30.420
Because of this, they cost
only 20% of the cost of the VM.

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:33.270
So they are significantly
cheaper than the regular VM.

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:35.880
And they're sort of guaranteed
to be preempted at least once

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:36.380
a day.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:43.080
So with Dataproc-- if you
didn't have a Dataproc,

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:45.690
you would have to deal
with these machines coming

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:47.130
and going.

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:49.830
You want your cluster
with 100 machines.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:52.980
You have 95 now, because
five of them were preempted.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:55.440
You have to find
another five, right?

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:58.500
With Dataproc, this is
managed for you automatically.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:00.720
So you can have your
Dataproc cluster

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:05.010
with the preemptible VMs
costing 1/5 of the compute

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:07.140
that you would have with
the Dataproc cluster

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:09.750
with the regular VMs.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:13.560
And another thing where
this is really useful

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.980
is if you separated your
storage from the compute,

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:20.042
the preemptible VMs don't
really cost you any loss.

00:20:20.042 --> 00:20:21.250
They just come and go, right?

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:31.070
Different computations
have different resource

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:32.370
requirements.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.520
So let's imagine that
our workload requires

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:38.510
six CPUs and eight gigs of RAM.

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:41.510
In Google, we have
standard instances,

00:20:41.510 --> 00:20:46.400
and one is n1-standard-4, which
comes with four CPUs and four

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:47.620
gigs of RAM.

00:20:47.620 --> 00:20:51.320
There is n1-highmem, which
comes with four CPUs and eight

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:52.940
gigs of RAM.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:56.000
And we have n1-standard-8,
which has eight CPUs

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.670
and 1-2-3-4-5-6-7--

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:00.200
8 gigs of RAM.

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:03.680
But we need six CPUs
and eight gigs of RAM.

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:07.670
So you can create
your Dataproc cluster

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:12.590
using custom VMs, which will
have six CPUs and whatever

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:13.820
RAM you need there.

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:17.540
I guess these are four gig
chips, so let's assume eight.

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:20.210
So depending on
computation you want to do,

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:24.470
you can actually make VMs fit
the size of your computation

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:26.750
and just run the
Dataproc cluster with it.

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:29.420
These custom VMs
came out in March

00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:34.580
and are a pretty neat
technological improvement.

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:39.580
You don't have to do any bin
packing of your workloads.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.760
So now we've seen--

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:44.520
we separated our data
from computation.

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:49.360
We understand that we can
have different size machines.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:53.020
So let's take this idea further.

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:59.520
We can right-size our clusters
based on the use case we have.

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:01.470
Think of the different
workloads you do.

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:02.699
You have a map reduce CTL.

00:22:02.699 --> 00:22:03.990
You have some machine learning.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:07.830
You do some high analytics,
and you do business reporting.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:13.000
Do all these workloads need
the same computational power?

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.100
Are some of them more
valuable than others,

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.350
and you're willing to
pay more for those VMs

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:20.030
so the things would
finish faster?

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:22.780
So if you were willing to
pay for business reporting

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:24.580
to use these high
performance VMs,

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:28.870
are you willing to spend
them on MapReduce CTL?

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:31.060
Perhaps, perhaps not.

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:35.620
So if you think of the
cluster as a building block,

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:37.540
you can understand
that here you have,

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:42.010
actually, potential for
different-shaped building

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:43.210
blocks.

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:45.730
So we can have four
clusters with the VMs that

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:48.970
look significantly different,
have a different price

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:52.060
performance label
attached to them,

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:54.220
and we can optimize for cost.

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:55.840
We can optimize for speed.

00:22:55.840 --> 00:23:01.130
And for different use case, we
can choose the right occasion.

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:07.940
So this is right-sizing
clusters for use case.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:11.140
But we can take it
one step further.

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:16.040
So we can have a thermal and
semi long-lived clusters.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:21.080
So at the beginning, I said
it takes us around 90 seconds

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:22.700
to start the Dataproc cluster.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:24.510
And we'll demo that soon.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:28.550
So this is pretty
immediate, right?

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.130
So we can choose the
lifetime of a cluster

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:34.250
according to the
workload it runs.

00:23:34.250 --> 00:23:36.630
On one end of the spectrum
are thermal clusters.

00:23:36.630 --> 00:23:39.987
Those are clusters that you
start to do this computation.

00:23:39.987 --> 00:23:41.570
And then when the
computation is done,

00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:45.020
you just leave them,
as simple as that.

00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:48.020
I know one of our partners
does exactly that.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:50.540
They have some custom
made Spark thing

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:54.170
that they run every night,
and it's pretty significant.

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:56.780
It uses around 1,000
cores or something.

00:23:56.780 --> 00:23:58.560
So every night at
midnight they spin up

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:01.150
1,000 core Dataproc cluster,
run the computation,

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:01.880
and tear it down.

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:10.730
And then following
night, they do the same.

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:13.940
It's all automated.

00:24:13.940 --> 00:24:15.440
The other thing
that's nice if you

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:17.450
treat the cluster
per computation,

00:24:17.450 --> 00:24:19.670
like in the thermal
mode, is imagine you

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:22.910
have to change a region
for whatever reason.

00:24:22.910 --> 00:24:24.410
It's essentially--
that all you have

00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:26.240
to do in that case
is, like, we'll

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:30.080
start a cluster in this
region and run jobs there.

00:24:30.080 --> 00:24:31.990
It's as simple as that.

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:33.640
On the other end
of the spectrum,

00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:36.970
you'll have the semi
long-lived clusters.

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:41.915
And even for them, they may
end up having some data there.

00:24:41.915 --> 00:24:44.290
Maybe you're running your own
Hive instance or something.

00:24:44.290 --> 00:24:46.770
So you really need them
there all the time.

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:49.110
They cannot really
go away easily.

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:52.360
Coming back to one of the
use cases at the beginning--

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:57.070
what does it take to
install the new version?

00:24:57.070 --> 00:24:59.320
Well, again, a few
clicks of a button

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:02.110
and selecting the preview
image for the latest

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:04.900
and the greatest, and
you start a new cluster.

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:06.940
You can start debugging on it.

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:10.130
You can start load testing
and everything else.

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:13.150
And once you are happy, just
migrate all your workflows

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:15.400
to this new cluster and
shut down the other one.

00:25:17.950 --> 00:25:22.400
So thermal clusters allow you
to really treat computation

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.860
very differently
than you did on-prem,

00:25:24.860 --> 00:25:26.000
but they're not required.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:30.830
It is something that can be done
if it makes your life simpler.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:34.640
If it doesn't, just go ahead
use the semi long-lived clusters

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:36.560
and benefit from
our ability to start

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:39.140
the clusters quickly when you
want to test the new versions.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:48.190
OK, so we settle on a couple
of architectural choices.

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:52.180
How do we actually manage
our cluster and our jobs?

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.780
So Dataproc exposes Rust API.

00:25:55.780 --> 00:25:58.330
And this standard
Rust API allows

00:25:58.330 --> 00:26:02.630
you to manage cluster, jobs,
and operations on the clusters.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:04.990
And we integrate with
many third party tools,

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:06.889
like mrjob, Luigi--

00:26:06.889 --> 00:26:07.930
I don't know which owns--

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:16.190
that allow you to sort of handle
your clusters using this API.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.190
You can also ssh
on your master node

00:26:18.190 --> 00:26:21.400
and use your standard
Hadoop and Spark commands

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:23.470
that you're used
to, but we're going

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:28.660
to see how managing Dataproc
through these APIs look like.

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:30.340
So can I have my computer?

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:36.500
OK, so here is the
cloud platform start

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:41.540
page, our console, and we'll
go find the Dataproc from here.

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:48.090
Dataproc is here.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.660
And this is in our demo project.

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:52.260
So there are four
projects right now.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:55.320
Two of them are in US,
and two are in Europe,

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:56.970
in different zones.

00:26:56.970 --> 00:27:00.120
And I start with
the Jelena Demo Zero

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.940
just so I make sure I
have one cluster running.

00:27:02.940 --> 00:27:05.250
And my colleagues
run the other ones.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:09.120
And we live in separate worlds.

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:11.760
I can't affect their
production or their demos,

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:13.740
and they can't
affect mine, which

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:18.090
is a really nice separation
sometimes to have.

00:27:18.090 --> 00:27:22.020
So I want to create a cluster.

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:25.937
And here we'll click
on Create a Cluster.

00:27:25.937 --> 00:27:27.020
And I'm going to call it--

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:33.520
I'm going to call it Two.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:35.680
I'm going to run it
in Europe, because we

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:37.270
are in Europe right now.

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:40.390
And I'm just going to
minimally configure it.

00:27:40.390 --> 00:27:42.940
I'm going to reduce
the size of the disks.

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:46.970
And I mentioned you could
try running a new version,

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:48.820
and that option exists here.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:51.190
So you can choose
the image version.

00:27:51.190 --> 00:27:54.246
And the preview on
is a nightly-built.

00:27:54.246 --> 00:27:55.370
We're not going to do that.

00:27:55.370 --> 00:27:56.911
We're just going to
keep the default.

00:27:56.911 --> 00:28:00.130
And I'm going to click Create.

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:07.230
So here we see my cluster
is starting to be made.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:12.520
And at the same time,
we'll do an experiment

00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:16.710
with the command
line, which is here.

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:19.190
And this is essentially
the command line.

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:22.830
We do gcloud Dataproc clusters.

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:27.150
Create Jelena Demo One
with the two workers.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.760
And this starts the operation.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:33.600
So here you can see two
clusters being spun up--

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.142
like, these two.

00:28:35.142 --> 00:28:36.320
It's readable, right?

00:28:40.814 --> 00:28:42.480
OK, and let's see how
long does it take.

00:28:45.860 --> 00:28:49.500
While they're spinning
up, we can look at them.

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:52.980
So here I already
have two workers.

00:28:52.980 --> 00:28:55.710
So this one probably
spun up fully.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:58.800
I can easily get some overview
on the CPU utilization

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:00.110
on what the workers--

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:01.530
the jobs are doing.

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:04.110
And I can see my configuration
at any given time.

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:09.270
I can check on the jobs,
but I didn't run any jobs.

00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:12.690
So there's nothing
there right now.

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:13.950
Let's go back to all of them.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:19.140
OK, so Demo Two started.

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:21.570
Demo One is still spinning up.

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:24.190
Let's check it out.

00:29:24.190 --> 00:29:26.910
Again, this one also
had only two instances,

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:29.993
and I'm guessing it's
essentially done.

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:39.000
OK, so let's open another tab,
and we'll go back to that one.

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:47.770
So now imagine we want to
run a job, and we'll run--

00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:53.330
I have here some Spark,
PySpark, hello world in PySpark.

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:57.100
It just starts a PySpark
and prints hello and world--

00:29:57.100 --> 00:30:04.340
nothing too exciting--
perfect for demo.

00:30:04.340 --> 00:30:08.760
OK, so our cluster started
in, like, 94 seconds.

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:09.510
[STOP WATCH BEEPS]

00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:12.630
So let's go back here.

00:30:12.630 --> 00:30:18.520
Let's run this job
using our APIs.

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:22.100
OK, so it copied the files.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:23.390
It started operation.

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:26.180
And this is running on Demo One.

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:29.130
So I can check the jobs there.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:30.900
It's running right now.

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:33.300
And the really cool thing
is that, essentially, you

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:43.865
can get the live stream of
the output in a few seconds.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:52.000
You can see it finished its job,
and it printed out hello world.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:56.870
Now, let's say we
wanted to run something

00:30:56.870 --> 00:31:02.210
a slightly bit more complicated
but still demo-worthy.

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:04.420
Let's compute the value of pi.

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:07.940
OK, and this one will
run another Spark job

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:13.460
with 1,000 tasks
to complete this.

00:31:13.460 --> 00:31:16.895
And this guy will run
on our Demo Two cluster.

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:28.370
OK, so you can see
small spikes in CPU,

00:31:28.370 --> 00:31:29.540
the jobs that are running--

00:31:29.540 --> 00:31:30.770
this one is running.

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:33.740
We have our VM instances.

00:31:33.740 --> 00:31:37.640
Again, if I click here,
I can see it executing,

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:43.330
and you can see this
is the number of tasks

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:44.030
it just started.

00:31:44.030 --> 00:31:47.110
So it has zero tasks,
and it completed zero.

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:50.110
And, I guess, the new
line is hard to add.

00:31:50.110 --> 00:31:54.260
So we'll have a little
bit of noisy output.

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:57.170
But we just computed pi.

00:31:57.170 --> 00:32:00.830
It's 3.14162.

00:32:00.830 --> 00:32:04.970
And for everyone who knows that
it's, like, 1-4-1-5-9 whatever,

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:06.920
we need to recompute it better.

00:32:06.920 --> 00:32:08.610
This is not good enough.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:13.750
So let's try running a
50 times bigger problem.

00:32:13.750 --> 00:32:24.760
So instead of 1,000 tasks,
let's do 50,000 tasks.

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:28.870
So this job with 1,000
tasks took 33 seconds.

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:31.540
We're now running
with 50,000 tasks.

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:36.100
And maybe we figure
out, whoops, this

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:39.124
is going to take 50
times 30 seconds--

00:32:39.124 --> 00:32:40.790
I have, like, seven
more minutes-- maybe

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:42.640
not a good idea.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:44.260
I need more resources.

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:46.830
So I'll edit the cluster.

00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:52.380
And instead of two,
I'll do 100 VMs.

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.910
OK, and I got quota--

00:32:58.910 --> 00:33:00.240
disk quota.

00:33:00.240 --> 00:33:06.060
OK, 200 is not going to
work, but let's do 14.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:21.710
Maybe I selected the
wrong zone, sorry.

00:33:21.710 --> 00:33:22.740
Come on.

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:24.350
OK, let's run it on
the other cluster.

00:33:28.510 --> 00:33:30.390
Actually, I'm really
upset with this cluster.

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:31.473
So I'm going to delete it.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:39.710
And that one is
going to go away,

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:41.210
and I'm just going
to kill this guy,

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:43.220
and go to the
beginning of the line,

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:45.020
and change it to
run on Demo One.

00:33:50.370 --> 00:33:53.220
And while we are doing
this, this cluster

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:56.760
is being deleted with all the
artifacts that it created.

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:59.910
And this one, I'm going to
increase to more machines,

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:02.480
because in this zone,
I know I have quota.

00:34:07.340 --> 00:34:09.420
Come on.

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:13.540
OK, so the quotas are not
cooperating with me today.

00:34:13.540 --> 00:34:15.090
So let's do 30 nodes.

00:34:20.199 --> 00:34:21.760
Thank you.

00:34:21.760 --> 00:34:23.810
So what does it mean?

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:24.850
Let's look at our VMs.

00:34:24.850 --> 00:34:26.199
Voila.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:28.989
My extra 30 VMs are
showing up, and it

00:34:28.989 --> 00:34:31.380
will take them a few seconds.

00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:34.560
And this is on my
Demo One cluster.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.389
And as they start
appearing, you'll

00:34:36.389 --> 00:34:39.570
see they're faster than
they were originally.

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:42.719
So in a few seconds
they're all here.

00:34:42.719 --> 00:34:44.550
And now all that
has to happen is

00:34:44.550 --> 00:34:51.100
that my Hadoop cluster
picks them up and starts

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:54.739
sending tasks to them, right?

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:57.400
And while the job
is running, I want

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:01.570
to show you that we allow you
to see your standard Hadoop UI.

00:35:01.570 --> 00:35:03.850
If you really, really,
really love your Hadoop UI,

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:10.570
and you know how to debug in
it, we allow you to do so.

00:35:10.570 --> 00:35:12.940
You just need to set up the VPN.

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:14.500
It's pretty straightforward.

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:16.400
It's documented on a page.

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:18.820
And on this guy, we
can check the nodes.

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:21.130
And at this point,
it started with two,

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:24.430
but now we have way more
nodes, and they're all

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:26.410
processing our job.

00:35:26.410 --> 00:35:30.820
OK, so with this, let's
switch back to the slides.

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:36.300
OK, so you've seen
me use a few ways

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:38.820
of accessing Rust APIs,
some from command line,

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:41.190
some from UI.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:44.150
It's integrated with the
tools like Luigi and mrjobs.

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:45.690
So you can use those
tools, as well,

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:48.810
to run the jobs on a Dataproc.

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:51.870
There are three ways you
can actually run the jobs.

00:35:51.870 --> 00:35:55.230
I did not demo the Hadoop one,
but essentially on that one

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:58.050
you would just
ssh on your master

00:35:58.050 --> 00:36:00.210
and be able to run
like you usually do.

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:06.560
You've seen me a few
times, unsuccessfully,

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:10.020
resize the cluster, but
then I was successful.

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:12.560
But the challenge was the quota.

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:15.500
I selected the wrong zone.

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:18.420
But the point is it
was a click away.

00:36:18.420 --> 00:36:23.390
And that's edit and change
the number of machines, right?

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:26.240
The thing I did not show you
all is that these clusters are

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:28.840
integrated with Stackdriver.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:31.800
Stackdriver allows you to set
up the monitoring and logging.

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:33.560
So essentially all
the information

00:36:33.560 --> 00:36:36.050
gathered about your cluster
will be preserved there,

00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:38.600
and it can be turned into
really nice dashboards.

00:36:38.600 --> 00:36:41.990
It can be set up such
that it causes alerts.

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:44.234
So if you have someone
monitoring your production,

00:36:44.234 --> 00:36:46.400
they can actually be alerted
if things are not going

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:48.050
the way they're supposed to.

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:50.810
And you'll be able to
access your logs even

00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:53.970
after you delete a cluster.

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:57.780
And to put it all
together, initially, we'll

00:36:57.780 --> 00:37:01.980
probably just start with moving
the data away and putting it

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:02.994
in a cloud.

00:37:02.994 --> 00:37:04.410
And then we'll
start experimenting

00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.950
with workloads that are maybe
bursty or new applications we

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:09.180
want to run.

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:11.490
As we go along, we
may end up building

00:37:11.490 --> 00:37:16.140
a hybrid infrastructure, where
you will host both your on-prem

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:18.700
and the GCP clusters.

00:37:18.700 --> 00:37:23.770
And finally, you might end up
in a native cloud state, where

00:37:23.770 --> 00:37:26.890
all your computations, all
your data lives in the cloud

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:29.500
and operates well with
all the other building

00:37:29.500 --> 00:37:33.070
blocks that we have in Google.

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:34.990
To start, we have a
good documentation

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:39.730
on cloud.google.com/dataproc.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:43.840
We also have many ways to get
help through our documentation,

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:45.460
through our release notes.

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:48.040
Stack overflow, we
scrub it all every day

00:37:48.040 --> 00:37:50.590
and make sure we
answer the questions.

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:54.310
We have email lists that
you can send emails to--

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:55.930
Google Cloud Support.

00:37:55.930 --> 00:38:00.700
And another one that is not
listed here is our partners.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:03.640
So if this looks
like a daunting task,

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:05.770
you can partner with
many good companies

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:09.400
that are listed on our website
that can help you with this.

00:38:09.400 --> 00:38:12.270
So with that, I'm happy
to take any questions.

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:15.240
[CLAPPING]

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:23.080
It seems like the
standard right here

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:25.160
is to ask questions
after the talk.

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:28.720
So thank you all for coming.

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:31.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

